WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.424
Let’s begin with values already present in ordinary Python code: a name, a

2
00:00:04.424 --> 00:00:09.147
message, or a list of items. Each one is a value in a Python program. Now widen

3
00:00:09.147 --> 00:00:13.810
the question. Do not ask only what the value is called. Ask what kind of thing

4
00:00:13.810 --> 00:00:18.414
it is, what information it currently holds, and what it knows how to do. That

5
00:00:18.414 --> 00:00:23.196
three-part question is our object model. A value has a type. Its current text or

6
00:00:23.196 --> 00:00:27.979
items are its state. Its type supplies behaviors, including methods. A string is

7
00:00:27.979 --> 00:00:32.343
therefore more than characters sitting in a variable. A list is more than

8
00:00:32.343 --> 00:00:36.827
several values between brackets. Both are objects that Python can work with

9
00:00:36.827 --> 00:00:41.610
according to their types. This is not yet a lesson about writing your own class.

10
00:00:41.610 --> 00:00:45.914
It is a way to look at values you already use. The name on the left is a

11
00:00:45.914 --> 00:00:50.278
reference we use to reach an object; the name itself is not the type. Two

12
00:00:50.278 --> 00:00:54.942
different strings can have different text and still have the same type. As you

13
00:00:54.942 --> 00:00:59.724
inspect each value, keep asking: what kind of object is this, what state does it

14
00:00:59.724 --> 00:01:01.936
hold, and what behavior can it offer?

15
00:01:01.936 --> 00:01:06.471
The next clue is a dot. When you see a value followed by a dot and a name,

16
00:01:06.471 --> 00:01:11.373
Python is looking up something attached to that object. When the name identifies

17
00:01:11.373 --> 00:01:16.214
a method, parentheses call that method. In spoken language, you can hear it as:

18
00:01:16.214 --> 00:01:21.116
ask this object to use this behavior. A string can be asked to become uppercase,

19
00:01:21.116 --> 00:01:25.712
remove outside spaces, or replace one piece of text. The parentheses matter

20
00:01:25.712 --> 00:01:30.369
because they perform the call; without them, you are referring to the method

21
00:01:30.369 --> 00:01:35.027
itself rather than asking it to run. Not every name after a dot is a method.

22
00:01:35.027 --> 00:01:39.439
Some are attributes that hold information. Today we are concentrating on

23
00:01:39.439 --> 00:01:44.341
methods, because they make behavior visible. The dot is not decoration and it is

24
00:01:44.341 --> 00:01:49.059
not a universal command. It is a route from an object to a named part of what

25
00:01:49.059 --> 00:01:53.533
that object offers. This is why methods feel as though they belong to the

26
00:01:53.533 --> 00:01:57.945
string: the string’s type supplies them. Pause and translate a line like

27
00:01:57.945 --> 00:02:02.602
“message dot upper with parentheses” into ordinary language: ask the message

28
00:02:02.602 --> 00:02:07.320
object for its uppercase behavior and call it. That translation will help you

29
00:02:07.320 --> 00:02:10.691
read programs before you can write every line yourself.

30
00:02:10.691 --> 00:02:15.419
Strings give us a useful surprise. A string method such as uppercase does not

31
00:02:15.419 --> 00:02:20.024
edit the original string in place. Strings are immutable, which means their

32
00:02:20.024 --> 00:02:24.814
contents cannot be changed in place. The method returns a string; the original

33
00:02:24.814 --> 00:02:29.480
is unchanged. CPython may hand back the same object when nothing changed, so

34
00:02:29.480 --> 00:02:34.331
compare values, not identity. If you call the method and throw away its result,

35
00:02:34.331 --> 00:02:38.875
the original reference still leads to the original text. If you assign the

36
00:02:38.875 --> 00:02:43.419
result back to the same variable, that variable now refers to the returned

37
00:02:43.419 --> 00:02:48.332
string. The important habit is to ask what came back. Some string methods return

38
00:02:48.332 --> 00:02:53.183
a string. The split method returns a list of pieces. The find method returns an

39
00:02:53.183 --> 00:02:57.726
integer position, while startswith returns a Boolean. A method call is not

40
00:02:57.726 --> 00:03:02.639
automatically an instruction that changes the object you started with. It may be

41
00:03:02.639 --> 00:03:07.551
a computation that produces a value. This distinction explains a common beginner

42
00:03:07.551 --> 00:03:12.279
error: saying that an uppercase call changes the string, then being surprised

43
00:03:12.279 --> 00:03:17.007
when printing the string shows the old text. Nothing mysterious happened. The

44
00:03:17.007 --> 00:03:21.674
call produced a result, and no one saved it. If you do save it, you have not

45
00:03:21.674 --> 00:03:26.218
repaired the old string; you have made your variable refer to the returned

46
00:03:26.218 --> 00:03:30.762
string. Think of a string method as sending a request to a text object and

47
00:03:30.762 --> 00:03:35.490
receiving a result in reply, then check the result's type instead of guessing

48
00:03:35.490 --> 00:03:37.025
from the receiver's type.

49
00:03:37.025 --> 00:03:41.681
Now compare a list. Lists are also objects, but a list is a mutable sequence.

50
00:03:41.681 --> 00:03:46.458
Mutable means the list can be changed in place. A method such as append adds an

51
00:03:46.458 --> 00:03:51.113
item to the existing list. The list after the call contains the new item. The

52
00:03:51.113 --> 00:03:55.587
return value is a different question: append returns None. That is why the

53
00:03:55.587 --> 00:04:00.243
useful result is usually the changed list, not a new list stored from append.

54
00:04:00.243 --> 00:04:05.020
The same pattern appears with other list methods that add, remove, or rearrange

55
00:04:05.020 --> 00:04:09.554
items. They act on the list itself. This is a place where type matters. The

56
00:04:09.554 --> 00:04:14.331
string method upper returns a string; the original is unchanged because strings

57
00:04:14.331 --> 00:04:19.047
are immutable. The list method append changes a mutable list. Neither behavior

58
00:04:19.047 --> 00:04:23.824
is the definition of all methods. It is a property you check for the particular

59
00:04:23.824 --> 00:04:28.600
type and method. A safe routine is: predict the object’s state before the call,

60
00:04:28.600 --> 00:04:33.074
predict whether the call returns a value, run it, and inspect both. If you

61
00:04:33.074 --> 00:04:37.730
assign the result of append to a variable, that variable holds None while the

62
00:04:37.730 --> 00:04:42.265
original list has changed. The list and the returned value are two separate

63
00:04:42.265 --> 00:04:43.716
things to keep track of.

64
00:04:43.716 --> 00:04:48.551
Let’s make the contrast concrete. With a string, call the method and print both

65
00:04:48.551 --> 00:04:53.447
the returned value and the original. You will see two different strings: the new

66
00:04:53.447 --> 00:04:58.159
uppercase text and the old lowercase text. With a list, call append, keep the

67
00:04:58.159 --> 00:05:02.933
returned value, and then print both. You will see None in the saved result and

68
00:05:02.933 --> 00:05:07.645
the added item in the original list. That pair of observations is more useful

69
00:05:07.645 --> 00:05:12.419
than memorizing a slogan such as strings return and lists mutate. It teaches a

70
00:05:12.419 --> 00:05:17.070
question: what does this method return, and does it change its receiver? The

71
00:05:17.070 --> 00:05:21.843
word receiver simply means the object before the dot, the object receiving the

72
00:05:21.843 --> 00:05:26.678
request. The answer comes from the type’s rules and the method’s documentation.

73
00:05:26.678 --> 00:05:31.329
A string can also return a list from split, so even a method on an immutable

74
00:05:31.329 --> 00:05:36.042
object may return a different type. A list can have methods such as copy that

75
00:05:36.042 --> 00:05:40.877
return a list, and it also has methods that change the list. Types set the menu

76
00:05:40.877 --> 00:05:45.711
of behaviors, but individual methods determine the details. Use the examples as

77
00:05:45.711 --> 00:05:50.546
tiny experiments. Predict first. Run second. Explain the printed lines in plain

78
00:05:50.546 --> 00:05:54.769
language. That loop turns an accidental result into object knowledge.

79
00:05:54.769 --> 00:05:56.519
Here is a small live debugging session. I start with `label = " python "` and

80
00:05:56.519 --> 00:05:58.292
write `clean = label.strip()`. Then I write `clean.upper()` and print `clean`.

81
00:05:58.292 --> 00:06:00.043
The output is still `python`, in lowercase. My first thought might be, “upper

82
00:06:00.043 --> 00:06:01.861
did not work,” but the evidence says something more precise: the call produced a

83
00:06:01.861 --> 00:06:03.657
string result, and I did not save it. I also used the parentheses correctly, so

84
00:06:03.657 --> 00:06:05.453
this is not the missing-parentheses bug. The repair is `clean = clean.upper()`,

85
00:06:05.453 --> 00:06:07.112
followed by `print(clean)`, which displays `PYTHON`. The first assignment

86
00:06:07.112 --> 00:06:08.817
changes what the name `clean` refers to after `strip` returns a string; the

87
00:06:08.817 --> 00:06:10.544
second assignment does the same after `upper` returns a string. The original

88
00:06:10.544 --> 00:06:12.295
string object was never edited in place. Now I make a neighboring list bug. I

89
00:06:12.295 --> 00:06:14.113
start with `route = ["red", "green"]`, write `saved = route.append("blue")`, and

90
00:06:14.113 --> 00:06:15.750
print `saved`. The output is `None`, but printing `route` shows `['red',

91
00:06:15.750 --> 00:06:17.455
'green', 'blue']`. That is not a failed append and not a new list hidden in

92
00:06:17.455 --> 00:06:19.250
`saved`. The list receiver changed in place, while append deliberately returned

93
00:06:19.250 --> 00:06:21.023
`None`. If I wanted a separate list value, I would use `copy`, as in `backup =

94
00:06:21.023 --> 00:06:22.842
route.copy()`, and then check that `backup` is a list. These two debugging cases

95
00:06:22.842 --> 00:06:24.638
look similar because both use a dot and parentheses, but their method contracts

96
00:06:24.638 --> 00:06:26.433
differ. I inspect the receiver, the saved return value, and the post-call state

97
00:06:26.433 --> 00:06:28.184
separately. That three-part check usually tells me whether I forgot to save a

98
00:06:28.184 --> 00:06:29.889
returned value, incorrectly expected a mutation, or simply called the wrong

99
00:06:29.889 --> 00:06:31.616
method. Before moving on, notice the variable names in this debugging story.

100
00:06:31.616 --> 00:06:33.366
`label`, `clean`, `route`, `saved`, and `backup` are references used to reach

101
00:06:33.366 --> 00:06:35.094
objects. They are not labels for permanent types. A name can be rebound to a

102
00:06:35.094 --> 00:06:36.822
returned string or a copied list, while the type of the object it reaches is

103
00:06:36.822 --> 00:06:38.549
determined by that current value. This is why printing a result and checking

104
00:06:38.549 --> 00:06:40.118
`type` can be more informative than reasoning from a variable's name.

105
00:06:40.118 --> 00:06:45.017
You do not need to memorize every method. Python can help you investigate. Type

106
00:06:45.017 --> 00:06:49.731
asks what kind of object you have. Dir lists names of attributes and methods

107
00:06:49.731 --> 00:06:54.321
available on that object. It is a menu, not a promise that every name is a

108
00:06:54.321 --> 00:06:59.096
method or that every method takes the same arguments. Help gives more focused

109
00:06:59.096 --> 00:07:03.996
documentation about a type or a method. A practical loop is: identify the type,

110
00:07:03.996 --> 00:07:08.896
look through the available names, ask for help on a promising method, predict a

111
00:07:08.896 --> 00:07:13.796
small result, and run a tiny experiment. Start with a familiar object such as a

112
00:07:13.796 --> 00:07:18.633
string or list, then try the same investigation on something new. If you see a

113
00:07:18.633 --> 00:07:23.161
method name in the list, read its help before guessing what it changes or

114
00:07:23.161 --> 00:07:27.999
returns. This is a professional programming habit. The goal is not to know the

115
00:07:27.999 --> 00:07:32.774
whole library in your head. The goal is to know how to ask a good question of

116
00:07:32.774 --> 00:07:37.302
the language and then test the answer. Documentation and experiments work

117
00:07:37.302 --> 00:07:41.953
together: documentation tells you the contract, while a small run helps you

118
00:07:41.953 --> 00:07:44.744
connect that contract to a value you can see.

119
00:07:44.744 --> 00:07:47.695
Let’s collect the evidence before the final review. An object is a value with a

120
00:07:47.695 --> 00:07:50.458
type, state, and available behavior. A variable name is a reference to the

121
00:07:50.458 --> 00:07:53.296
current object, so the name does not tell us the whole story. A dot performs

122
00:07:53.296 --> 00:07:56.283
attribute lookup, and parentheses call a method; without the parentheses we have

123
00:07:56.283 --> 00:07:59.196
only referred to the method attribute. For strings, immutability means methods

124
00:07:59.196 --> 00:08:01.885
such as `upper`, `strip`, and `replace` return a string; the original is

125
00:08:01.885 --> 00:08:04.611
unchanged. CPython may hand back the same object when nothing changed, so

126
00:08:04.611 --> 00:08:07.523
compare values, not identity. `split` is a useful reminder that the result can

127
00:08:07.523 --> 00:08:10.287
be a list, while `find` gives an integer position and `startswith` gives a

128
00:08:10.287 --> 00:08:13.125
Boolean. For lists, mutability allows in-place changes. `append` changes the

129
00:08:13.125 --> 00:08:16.038
receiver and returns `None`; `copy` returns a list. There is no universal “all

130
00:08:16.038 --> 00:08:18.988
methods return” or “all methods mutate” rule. The method’s documentation is the

131
00:08:18.988 --> 00:08:21.826
contract. When a line surprises you, debug it in a fixed order. Identify the

132
00:08:21.826 --> 00:08:24.776
receiver and its type. Read the call carefully, including its parentheses. Save

133
00:08:24.776 --> 00:08:27.688
the return value if you need it. Inspect the receiver after the call. Then use

134
00:08:27.688 --> 00:08:30.676
`dir` to discover names, `help` to read focused documentation, and a tiny run to

135
00:08:30.676 --> 00:08:33.514
test one prediction. This routine makes the invisible parts of a method call

136
00:08:33.514 --> 00:08:36.352
visible: the object before the dot, the result sent back, and the state that

137
00:08:36.352 --> 00:08:39.227
remains afterward. You are not memorizing isolated tricks. You are building a

138
00:08:39.227 --> 00:08:40.908
repeatable way to learn an unfamiliar object.

139
00:08:40.908 --> 00:08:45.627
The review is complete. We met values we already use, followed the dot, and

140
00:08:45.627 --> 00:08:50.284
compared what string and list methods do. The reusable habit is this: when

141
00:08:50.284 --> 00:08:54.878
Python gives you a thing, ask what type it has, what state it holds, what

142
00:08:54.878 --> 00:08:59.724
behavior it offers, what it returns, and whether it changes in place. Strings

143
00:08:59.724 --> 00:09:04.758
and lists are not merely containers for syntax practice. They are early examples

144
00:09:04.758 --> 00:09:09.666
of a much larger way to organize software. Later, objects may represent files,

145
00:09:09.666 --> 00:09:14.449
paths, game entities, or connections. The exact methods will come from their

146
00:09:14.449 --> 00:09:19.295
types and documentation, so do not assume that a conceptual example is a real

147
00:09:19.295 --> 00:09:24.329
API. For today, keep the contrast sharp. Uppercase on a string returns a string;

148
00:09:24.329 --> 00:09:29.237
the original is unchanged. Append on a list changes the list and returns None.

149
00:09:29.237 --> 00:09:34.020
Dir and help make unfamiliar behavior discoverable. If you can explain those

150
00:09:34.020 --> 00:09:39.054
observations, you already have a working first model of objects. The next time a

151
00:09:39.054 --> 00:09:43.963
method surprises you, do not guess that all objects behave like strings or all

152
00:09:43.963 --> 00:09:48.871
methods behave like append. Identify the type, read the method’s contract, and

153
00:09:48.871 --> 00:09:50.759
run the smallest useful check.
